Resonance, layer by layer

Resonance runs real binaries in isolated network namespaces and checks
that they converge as a cluster while the network misbehaves. A
network namespace (netns) is a separate network stack: its own
interfaces, addresses, and routes, cut off from the host. A TUN
device is a virtual network card that hands packets to user code
instead of a wire. Resonance was built for Concord, and Weld uses
the same machinery for ROS and DDS testing.

If you are new, read this file first, then the layer files in order.
Each layer file explains one part of the system: what it is, why it
exists, and which source files implement it.

The six layers

Every scenario does the same six things. The layers are the same six
things, split apart so each can be reused on its own.

1. Simulation core (/docs/layer0-sim). A clock, an event queue,
   a node list, a radio world, a random generator, and in-process UDP.
   No processes, no network, no binaries. Pure data structures with
   deterministic replay.
2. Mesh transport (/docs/layer1-mesh). Real packets through real
   TUN devices, gated by the simulated radio. This is how unmodified
   binaries talk to each other through a fake physical world.
3. Process lifecycle (/docs/layer2-lifecycle). Starting,
   killing, and restarting real binaries inside network namespaces,
   with five different restart meanings (fresh, preserved, re-keyed,
   wiped key, wiped state).
4. Fault control (/docs/layer3-faults). Dropping packets,
   blinding multicast, moving nodes out of radio range, slowing links,
   and killing processes. All faults work from outside the binary.
5. Observation (/docs/layer4-observation). Reading child logs,
   journals on disk, and CLI output to decide what the cluster did.
6. Scenario runner (/docs/layer5-runner). The poll loop, the
   phase machine, the timers, teardown, and the verdict.

Speed is its own topic (/docs/speed). The short version: some
slowness is the harness and can be fixed, and some slowness is the
binary's own timers and cannot be fixed from outside. That file tells
the two apart. Terms are defined in docs/glossary.md.

Where the code lives

run/ holds the shared runner used by new scenarios: setup, the
poll loop, holds, throttling, teardown, and the verdict plumbing.
driver/ holds per-system verbs: driver/concord.h for Concord
spawn, kill, restart, submit, and queries, plus driver/weld.h for
weld echo nodes, ROS peers, and publish. suite/ holds the YAML
parser (suite/parse.h) and the executor (suite/exec.h) that maps
phases to runner calls. cmd/rsuite is the CLI that lints, lists,
runs, and checks the setup. scenarios/*.yaml and
suite/examples/*.yaml are the YAML scenarios the CLI runs.

The two run paths

check/ scenarios each carry their own copied poll loop. That loop
is frozen: 26 files carry a byte-identical hold() helper and the
rest use the same pattern inline, and new
behavior goes beside them, never by editing them. YAML scenarios
take the other path: bin/rsuite run forks one child per file, and
each child runs suite_run_file (suite/exec.h) on the shared
run/ loop with a fully isolated network setup. Same mesh, same
faults, one shared loop instead of copies.

How one check/ scenario runs

Here is the whole story in ten steps, using check/concord_three.c
as the check/-only example. Three nodes mesh, one gets isolated,
then it rejoins.

1. Check that ./bin/concord exists. If not, skip quietly.
2. Build the simulation core: clock at zero, empty node list, empty
   radio grid.
3. Create one network namespace per extra node.
4. Open one TUN device per node (node 0 in the host namespace, the
   rest inside their own namespace).
5. Register the nodes and give them underlay addresses
   (192.168.100.1/2/3, a range kept disjoint from the overlay
   10.0.0.0/16 the binaries use among themselves). The underlay
   is the harness's own wiring between TUN devices. The overlay is
   the address range the binaries form their own mesh on, never
   seeing the underlay.
6. Fork one real Concord binary per node, each with its own config
   home, and collect their log output through pipes.
7. Poll in a loop: pump packets between TUNs through the radio check,
   and scan log lines for "peers":3.
8. Once meshed and settled, stop pumping one node's packets
   (read and discard instead of forward). That node is partitioned.
9. After a hold time, resume pumping. Watch for "peers":3 again
   plus the peer.lost marker from the partition window.
10. Kill everything, free memory, delete namespaces, wipe state,
    and return pass or fail.

Steps 2 to 5 are Layer 1. Step 6 is Layer 2. Steps 8 and 9 are
Layer 3. Step 7's log scanning is Layer 4, and the loop holding it
all together is Layer 5. A YAML scenario runs the same steps
through suite_run_file: phases with do steps for faults,
until plus hold_s for waits, and sustain for holds that must
never break.

Two rules for the whole codebase

- check/ is frozen. Existing scenarios are read-only. New behavior
  is added beside them, never by editing them.
- The mesh is the only channel between nodes. Anything that bypasses
  the mesh (the image push in img/ and the docker ROS-peer control
  path in driver/weld.h) must be declared and
  documented, never silent.

Running the checks

  make                    # build ./bin/resonance
  make check              # fast checks, no Concord binary needed
  sudo make check-full    # all check/ scenarios, needs ./bin/concord
  make suite-lint         # validate scenarios/*.yaml + suite/examples/*.yaml, no privileges
  bin/rsuite lint file.yaml...    # same validation, explicit files
  bin/rsuite run file.yaml...     # run YAML scenarios (needs root for netns + TUN)
  bin/rsuite list file.yaml...    # parse-only summary, needs no privileges
  bin/rsuite doctor                 # report on binaries, TUN, netns, docker, run lock

make check needs no privileges for most binaries. The two TUN tests
skip gracefully without them. bin/rsuite run needs root because
it creates network namespaces and TUN devices.
